    <origin>Route authorization sweep of 2026-10-02 (projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/route-authorization-sweep-2026-10-02.md), confirmed findings #9 (POST /api/dataverse/fetch, critical) and #10 (GET /api/dataverse/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id}, critical). Both were confirmed by an independent skeptic who found no missed guard. Owner round 9 (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md, 2026-10-03): UAC-r2 fixes all 82 findings now, and the read fix pattern is "the caller's OWN Dataverse rights decide (D1, C9)", through the existing DataverseImpersonation helper or a CallerRecordAccessProbe pre-check, failing closed with unknown and denied ids answered the same. No open task or GitHub issue covered these routes: task 003 (POML line 116) put DataverseAuthorizationFilter out of scope, and task 134 (POML line 110) forbids changing the internal /api/dataverse surface.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Any signed-in workforce user who holds Read on an entity at ANY depth (User/Basic is enough, and the Basic User role grants it on most tables) can call POST /api/dataverse/fetch with any FetchXML, or GET /api/dataverse/record/{entity}/{id} with any id and $select. The BFF then runs the query with its own application identity, so the caller gets every row in the org and every column: secure records, other business units, records behind a No Access entry, and field-level-secured columns. Make both routes execute in Dataverse AS THE CALLER, so the caller gets exactly the rows and columns Dataverse lets that user read, and fail closed everywhere. Keep the response shape and every client consumer working, and do not touch the external contact seam that shares the same two services.</prompt>

  <amendments source="main session, 2026-10-03, owner rounds 9-10" binding="true">
    <!-- Read these before step 0. Each overrides anything below that contradicts it. -->
    <amendment>Owner decisions now run to round 10: re-read notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md rounds 9 and 10 on work/unified-access-control-r2 before step 0. They are newer than this POML.</amendment>
    <amendment>Do NOT rename or move FinanceAuthorizationFilter, and do not create a RecordAccessAuthorizationFilter or any other generalized copy of it: reuse it as it is. The rename is follow-up task 170, after the sweep tasks are integrated. Several sweep tasks reuse this filter in parallel, and a move in one of them would conflict with all the others.</amendment>
    <amendment>Task 167 (the every-route guard) runs IN PARALLEL with this task. If RouteAuthorizationGuardTests on your branch has no Pending waiver for your routes (167 not yet landed), do NOT add waivers, ledger entries or Permanent waivers, and do NOT add your files to GovernedFiles. Instead, add a table "Route authorization ledger input" to your task note, with one row per route you changed or deleted: the route key exactly as the guard spells it (VERB /path), the mechanism that now decides (filter, probe or impersonation), the deny test (its fully qualified name, proving a caller without rights is refused), and, for a list whose query runs AS the caller, the words "handler decision: query runs as the caller". The main session records these in 167's ledger at integration. If 167 HAS landed on your branch, follow its ledger contract: delete your Pending waivers and set ResolvedBy and ProofTest in the same diff.</amendment>
    <amendment>Owner round 10 item 1 (BINDING): delete a route when it has NO caller in the repo AND it is NOT in any published API description; fix it otherwise. Search the repo for OpenAPI documents and Copilot or agent plugin manifests (for example *openapi*, ai-plugin*, *manifest*.json, declarativeAgent*) before you decide. List every deletion, with its no-caller and not-published evidence, in the note and the PR. Delete the services, filters and options that only the deleted routes used. Keep the file census count in RouteAuthorizationGuardTests right if a whole endpoint file goes.</amendment>
    <amendment>Impersonation: never assign ServiceClient.CallerId or CallerAADObjectId outside Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseImpersonation.cs (task 160 adds the single SDK entry point there, plus an arch rule). For the Web API, impersonate through DataverseWebApiService's existing impersonateSystemUserId parameter (MSCRMCallerID). It refuses Guid.Empty (task 104).</amendment>
    <amendment>Owner round 10 item 1 applies to BOTH routes here (POST /api/dataverse/fetch and GET /api/dataverse/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id}). If neither has a caller in the repo and neither is in a published API description, DELETE them (and their now-unused FetchService / RecordService / filter mode) instead of building the impersonation fix. Deliver the single SDK impersonation entry point and its arch rule ONLY if a production caller needs it now (CLAUDE.md §11: no unused API). If you delete the routes and no caller needs SDK impersonation, record that tasks 159-166 use the Web API path.</amendment>
  </amendments>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer: BFF Minimal API endpoints (ADR-001/ADR-008), the Dataverse SDK ServiceClient, Dataverse impersonation (CallerId / MSCRMCallerID) and its fault codes, ProblemDetails (ADR-019), and fail-closed authorization (ADR-003).</role>

  <goal>After this task:
(1) POST /api/dataverse/fetch runs the caller's FetchXML on a ServiceClient clone impersonating the caller's Dataverse systemuserid. A caller gets only the rows (and only the columns) Dataverse returns to that user. A FetchXML that names a row the caller cannot read returns that row nowhere: the answer is the same as for a row that does not exist.
(2) GET /api/dataverse/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id} retrieves the record the same way. A record the caller cannot read and a record that does not exist return the IDENTICAL 404.
(3) The caller's identity comes from the existing CallerRecordAccessProbe.GetCallerSystemUserIdAsync (OBO WhoAmI, which cannot name anyone but the caller). If it cannot be resolved, nothing is executed and the request is refused.
(4) There is no app-only fallback anywhere on these two routes. Impersonation that Dataverse refuses is an error response, never a retry as the app.
(5) The external module seam (/api/v1/external/api/dataverse/*, ExternalModuleDataEndpoints.cs), which deliberately calls the same FetchService and RecordService app-only for contacts, is unchanged.
(6) The response shape of both routes is unchanged for an authorized caller, so BffDataverseClient and its tests need no change.
(7) Every new guard is proven to bite by seeding. The real per-resource control (the handlers calling only the caller-scoped methods) is pinned by this task's own arch guard, independent of task 167, and the route guard never credits these routes merely because the entity-level DataverseAuthorizationFilter is attached.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 LIVE. Every anchor below was read on the work branch on 2026-10-02 (adversarially re-checked the same day). Line numbers drift: re-verify at Step 0.

THE TWO ROUTES.
• POST /api/dataverse/fetch — `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/Dataverse/FetchEndpoints.cs`. Group `/api/dataverse` with a bare `.RequireAuthorization()` (`:50-52`), route `:54`, filter `.AddDataverseAuthorizationFilter(EntitySource.FromFetchXmlBody)` (`:67`), handler `ExecuteFetchAsync` (`:72-217`), service call `fetchService.ExecuteAsync(request, ct)` (`:166`). Its remarks (`:15-45`) and `.WithDescription` (`:57-60`) describe the filter as the whole security model and say the singleton ServiceClient is reused for speed (`:38-39`). The entity-mismatch check (`:126-146`) checks shape only. Existing catches: `InvalidOperationException` → 500 `DV_FETCH_SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE` (`:188-202`), anything else → 500 `DV_FETCH_INTERNAL_ERROR` (`:203-216`); neither echoes the exception message.
• GET /api/dataverse/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id:guid} — `Api/Dataverse/RecordEndpoints.cs`. Same group shape (`:40-42`), route `:44`, filter `.AddDataverseAuthorizationFilter(EntitySource.FromRouteValueWithRecord)` (`:48`), handler `GetRecordAsync` (`:53-171`), service call `recordService.GetRecordAsync(...)` (`:94-96`). Its catches, in order: `RecordNotFoundException` → 404 whose detail is `ex.Message`, which contains the entity name and the id (`:104-118`); `ArgumentException` → 400 that ECHOES `ex.Message` (`:119-133`); `Exception when IsInvalidSelectField(ex.Message)` → 400 that ECHOES `ex.Message` (`:139-155`, heuristic at `:197-204`); anything else → 500 (`:156-170`). The remarks at `:20-24` and `.WithDescription` at `:47` CLAIM row-level security via an impersonated CallerId. That is false.
• Both are mapped unconditionally: `Program.cs:264-265`; services registered at `Program.cs:82-83` (`Services/Dataverse/Extensions/FetchServiceExtensions.cs:39`, `RecordServiceExtensions.cs:21`, both `AddScoped`). Both service classes are `internal sealed` (`FetchService.cs:39`, `RecordService.cs:29`). `Sprk.Bff.Api.csproj:43,51` grants InternalsVisibleTo to `Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests` and `Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests`.

WHY THEY LEAK.
• `Services/Dataverse/DataverseAuthorizationFilter.cs:136-157` checks only that the caller's readable-entity SET contains each entity name. `Services/Dataverse/Privileges/UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:121-193` builds that set from `RetrieveUserPrivileges`, keeping any `prvRead*` privilege by name, at any depth (Basic/Local/Deep/Global). No record is consulted. For `FromRouteValueWithRecord`, `ResolveEntities` (`:183-193`) shares the `FromRouteValue` case and never reads `{id}`; the enum doc at `:29-32` suggests otherwise.
• `Services/Dataverse/FetchService.cs:82` unwraps the singleton app-identity ServiceClient (`UnwrapServiceClient`) and `:95-96` runs `RetrieveMultipleAsync(new FetchExpression(...))` with no CallerId. `ProjectEntity` (`:141-171`) returns every attribute, including `AliasedValue` from joins, plus `@formattedValues` and `@logicalName`. Its remarks (`:27-32`) say "every entity has been verified", which is entity-level only.
• `Services/Dataverse/RecordService.cs:74` calls `IDataverseService.RetrieveAsync` (app-only singleton). Its remarks (`:22-27`) claim the impersonated CallerId path; there is none. Not-found mapping is by MESSAGE TEXT (`:91-105`, `IsRecordNotFound` `:204-210`). `ResolveDefaultColumnsAsync` (`:113-163`) reads entity METADATA app-only, which is schema, not record data; for an unknown entity it throws `RecordNotFoundException(entity, Guid.Empty)` (`:156-162`).
• The only `CallerId` assignment in `src/server` is `UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:147-148` (clone, then `impersonated.CallerId = systemUserId`), used only to read the caller's privileges. That clone path is exercised LIVE on every privilege-cache miss, which is evidence that `ServiceClient.Clone()` works with the BFF's credential. (Grep 2026-10-02 for `CallerId =`, `.CallerId`, `CallerAADObjectId` across `src/server/**/*.cs`: that one site, plus a doc-comment cref at `UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:21`.)

THE EXISTING MACHINERY TO REUSE (read each before writing code).
• `src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseImpersonation.cs` (task 104). Web API only: `ApplyAsSystemUser(HttpRequestMessage, Guid)` (`:64-74`) and `ApplyAsEntraUser(...)` (`:90-116`) refuse `Guid.Empty` and stamp exactly one header (`:119-124`). It has NO entry point for the SDK `ServiceClient`. Spaarke.Dataverse already references `Microsoft.PowerPlatform.Dataverse.Client` 1.2.26 (`Spaarke.Dataverse.csproj:18`), so an SDK entry point adds no package. ADR-028 Amendment A5 (`.claude/adr/ADR-028-spaarke-auth-architecture.md:298-305`) states: "Any new access-scoped impersonated path MUST carry its own equivalent refusal."
• `src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseWebApiService.cs` `RetrieveMultipleImpersonatedAsync` (`:1030-1067`) is the Web API sibling. It returns OData JSON rows (`Dictionary&lt;string, JsonElement&gt;`), which is a DIFFERENT wire shape from `FetchService.ProjectEntity` (SDK `EntityReference` / `OptionSetValue` / `Money` / `AliasedValue` objects plus `@formattedValues`) and from `RecordService.ProjectEntityToDictionary`. Moving these routes onto it would change both routes' response shape. That is why this task extends the helper with an SDK entry point rather than switching transports (see escalation trigger 2).
• `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerRecordAccessProbe.cs` `GetCallerSystemUserIdAsync(callerBearerToken, ct)` (`:342-366`, public virtual). It resolves the caller's systemuserid by `WhoAmI()` under an OBO token (`ResolveCallerSystemUserIdAsync`, `:567-605`), which "cannot name anyone but the caller", returns null on every failure, and never returns `Guid.Empty` (`:589-593`). It carries no not-found backoff (that is on `GetCallerRightsAsync` only). Registered unconditionally as a typed HttpClient (`Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs:105-125`); without OBO configuration it returns null (fail closed). Precedents that use it to learn who the caller is: `Api/ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:719-735` (with `TokenHelper.ExtractBearerTokenOrNull(httpContext)`, `Infrastructure/Auth/TokenHelper.cs:42`) and `Api/ExternalAccess/UnsecureProjectEndpoint.cs:329-331`. Test-double precedent: `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/DelegationRuleTestFixture.cs:126-133` (registration) and `:219-235` (`StubCallerRecordAccessProbe : CallerRecordAccessProbe`).
• `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Dataverse/RecordContainerResolver.cs:1915-1941` `internal static bool IsRecordNotFound(Exception ex)` — the EXISTING code-based (not message-based) not-found predicate: `FaultException&lt;OrganizationServiceFault&gt;` with `Detail.ErrorCode == -2147220969` (0x80040217). Its remarks say message matching fails on localized orgs and that "a second copy of this predicate is how the two would drift". This task's classifier REUSES it for not-found; it does not copy the constant.
• Rejected identity sources, so they are not re-litigated: `Services/Identity/SystemUserIdentityResolver.cs` (cached `azureactivedirectoryobjectid` lookup); `Services/Ai/Context/CallerSystemUserResolver.cs` (`ICallerSystemUserResolver`, oid claim → `systemuser.azureactivedirectoryobjectid`; it is what the Office `GET /api/office/search/entities` fix uses, RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs:654-660); and the private lookup in `UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:200-233`. The probe's own remarks (`CallerRecordAccessProbe.cs:330-340`) explain why a second, oid-based identity path is the weaker choice: it can silently map to the wrong user or miss.
• Rejected transport: `Infrastructure/Dataverse/IDataverseUserClient.cs` (OBO Web API). It is correct in principle but returns Web API JSON, so it has the same wire-shape problem as above.
• ADR-028 plane note (so adr-check does not misfire): A1/A2/A3's "no OBO" rule governs the broker-only Teams/SPA plane (`/api/v1/external/**`). `/api/dataverse/*` is a workforce route; the probe's OBO WhoAmI already runs on workforce routes (`DelegationRuleFilter`, `RecordRouteAccessAuthorizationFilter`, `ProvisionProjectEndpoint`). The data read itself is A5-style: the BFF's own credential plus a caller identity, not the caller's token.

THE SHARED-SERVICE HAZARD (the client consumer that must not break).
`Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalModuleDataEndpoints.cs` maps `/api/v1/external/api/dataverse/fetch` and `/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id:guid}` (`:122-140`) and passes the METHOD GROUPS `fetchService.ExecuteAsync` (`:180-189`) and `recordService.GetRecordAsync` (`:312-320`) as delegates. Its header (`:30-44`) says those reads are APP-ONLY by design (ADR-028 A1/A2/A3 broker-only), scoped by Tier-2 FetchXML injection, the per-module column allow-list (task 134) and `IsRecordAccessible`, and that its stripping lives there "because those are shared with the internal /api/dataverse surface". A CIAM contact is not a security principal and cannot be impersonated (project CLAUDE.md fact 4). Making the EXISTING `ExecuteAsync` / `GetRecordAsync` impersonate would therefore break every external SPA grid. The fix adds caller-scoped entry points with DIFFERENT NAMES (not overloads) next to them, so those method-group conversions stay unambiguous, and leaves the app-only ones byte-identical.

CLIENT CONSUMERS (inventory at authoring; the executor re-runs it).
• `src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/BffDataverseClient.ts` is the only client code that builds these URLs: `retrieveMultipleRecords` → `POST {bffBaseUrl}/api/dataverse/fetch` (`:359-391`) and `retrieveRecord` → `GET {bffBaseUrl}/api/dataverse/record/...` (`:397-415`). It documents 400/403/404/5xx mapping to typed errors (`:24-31`). Its tests: `src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/__tests__/BffDataverseClient.test.ts`.
• Its ONLY instance in `src` is `src/client/external-spa/src/services/gridDataverseClient.ts:70`, whose `bffBaseUrl` is `{BFF_API_URL}/api/v1/external` (`:64`). That targets the EXTERNAL seam, not these routes.
• Every in-repo internal DataGrid host passes `XrmDataverseClient` (host-context `Xrm.WebApi`, Dataverse-enforced): `src/solutions/CommunicationReconciliation/src/main.tsx:222`, `src/solutions/EmailPage/src/main.tsx:404`, `src/solutions/DocumentUploadWizard/src/services/codePageDataverseClient.ts:129`, `src/solutions/LegalWorkspace/src/sections/reconciliation.registration.ts:53`, `email.registration.ts:77`, `src/client/pcf/RecordHeader/control/useHeaderFormMetadata.ts:505`, `src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/DataGrid/DataGrid.tsx:787` (the default) and `DataGridPageShell.tsx:312`, `src/client/shared/Spaarke.AI.Widgets/src/widgets/workspace/DataverseEntityViewWidget.tsx:189`, `ReconciliationWorkspaceWidget.tsx:215`, `EmailWorkspaceWidget.tsx:153`, `MetricsDashboardWidget.tsx:363`, `src/client/shared/Spaarke.Events.Components/src/widgets/CalendarWorkspaceWidget/CalendarWorkspaceWidget.tsx:752`. Storybook stories pass mock clients.
• The Copilot agent OpenAPI (`src/solutions/CopilotAgent/spaarke-bff-openapi.yaml`) names neither route.
• So at authoring there is NO in-repo caller of the internal routes. Out-of-repo callers (Power Automate, a Copilot agent, a script) are unknown. The DataGrid framework documents BffDataverseClient for non-MDA hosts (`docs/architecture/SPAARKE-DATAGRID-FRAMEWORK-ARCHITECTURE.md:32`, `:97`), so a future internal host would call these routes. A DataGrid loads its own `sprk_gridconfiguration` record through `retrieveRecord` before anything else (`Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs:410-417` describes that flow), so the caller needs row-level Read on that `sprk_gridconfiguration` record once the read is impersonated: a live-gate check.

THE ROUTE GUARD (task 074 / 167).
• `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs` does NOT govern `Api/Dataverse/*.cs` today (no GovernedFiles entry; `:99-101` says everything else is NotGoverned), so these two routes are outside it. Task 167 (owner round 9 item 3) extends the guard to every route file and adds a Pending waiver per known finding. At authoring (2026-10-02), task 167's POML is not on disk (no tasks/159-167 files).
• ⚠️ The guard is BLIND to this defect class as written: `FilterMarker` (`:1898-1902`, pattern `\.Add\w*AuthorizationFilter\s*[&lt;(]`) matches `.AddDataverseAuthorizationFilter(` as a per-resource gate (Rule A), and Rule B accepts the filter because `DecisionServices` lists `"IDataversePrivilegeChecker"` with the comment "DataverseAuthorizationFilter" (`:1287`). Once 167 governs these files as RouteLevelGate, both routes read GATED before any fix. So 167 cannot honestly add a Pending waiver for them: it would be STALE on arrival (`NoWaiverIsStale`, `:988-`). That is why this task pins the real control with its OWN arch guard (rule 2 below) rather than relying on 167.
• Precedent for recording that an impersonated query is the gate: the Office `GET /api/office/search/entities` Permanent waiver "RESOLVED … authorization is the IMPERSONATED query itself" (`:640-663`, #1021), and the three #1020 waivers (`:610-640`). Maintenance rules: `:348-367` (rule 4: never add a Permanent waiver to make a build green).

TESTS THAT EXIST.
• `tests/integration/Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests/Api/Dataverse/FetchEndpointTests.cs` and `RecordEndpointTests.cs` run the REAL host through `DataverseIntegrationTestFixture.cs`: Moq'd `IDataverseService` (`:84`, registered `:240-241`), Moq'd `IDataversePrivilegeChecker` (`:77`, registered `:245-246`), real `IFetchXmlEntityExtractor` (`:91`, registered `:250-251`), and `DataverseFakeAuthHandler` (`:308-346`), which authenticates any non-empty Authorization header. Because the fixture's mock fails `UnwrapServiceClient`, FetchService happy paths surface as 500 there today (fixture comment `:235-239`). RecordEndpointTests' happy paths (`:68-130`) and its `Verify` at `:119` depend on the app-only `IDataverseService.RetrieveAsync` mock. Helper: `tests/integration/Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests/Helpers/MockServiceClientFactory.cs`.
• `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/DataverseImpersonationHelperTests.cs` (task 104 helper tests, compiled into the BFF unit suite via `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests.csproj:111`).
• `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/ImpersonationNegativeCanaryTests.cs` (NFR-04: an impersonated low-privilege read must return strictly FEWER rows than app-only; equality fails; its row comparison is live-tenant only).
• External-seam tests that must stay green unmodified (all compiled into the BFF unit suite): `tests/integration/contract/Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalModuleDataContractTests.cs`, `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/FetchXmlGuardSelfJoinTests.cs`, `ExternalModuleColumnAllowListTests.cs`, `ExternalScopeCharacterizationTests.cs`.
• Source-scan precedent with a single allowed file and a seeded negative control: `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/DataverseServiceClientDowncastTests.cs` (helpers in `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/SourceScan.cs`: `ServerSourceFiles`, `StripLineComment`, `CodeText`).

DATAVERSE FAULT CODES (stated here so the classifier is not guessed). As `OrganizationServiceFault.ErrorCode` (signed 32-bit): ObjectDoesNotExist 0x80040217 = -2147220969 (already pinned by `RecordContainerResolver.cs:1919`); record-level access denied, "SecLib::AccessCheckEx failed", 0x80048306 = -2147187962 — this is what a Retrieve of an existing row the impersonated user cannot read normally returns; missing table privilege, "Principal user … is missing prvRead… privilege", 0x80040220 = -2147220960; CannotActOnBehalfOfAnotherUser 0x8004A110 (`DataverseImpersonation.cs:44-46`). The two denial codes are from common Dataverse experience, not from code in this repo: live-gate item (i) confirms them, and any other code observed for an unreadable row is added to the classifier before the task is marked done. The existing comment at `RecordService.cs:100` ("Some Dataverse SDK code paths surface not-found as a non-typed Exception") means the executor must also confirm, at Step 0 from the Dataverse.Client 1.2.26 behaviour and at the live gate, which exception TYPE carries the fault for `RetrieveAsync` / `RetrieveMultipleAsync` on a cloned client.

LIVE PREREQUISITE. Impersonation needs the BFF application user to hold `prvActOnBehalfOfAnotherUser`. In dev it comes through System Administrator on `# mi-bff-api-dev`, `SDAP-BFF-SPE-API` and `# Spaarke DMS-SPE Dev 1` (session 27 "Live facts", checked by role, not by a live call). Without it Dataverse returns CannotActOnBehalfOfAnotherUser (0x8004A110) rather than widening access (`DataverseImpersonation.cs:44-46`).</background>
