  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Re-verify every anchor in the background (line numbers drift). Check whether task 167 has landed on this branch (constraint "the route guard"). Establish, from the Dataverse.Client 1.2.26 behaviour, which exception type `RetrieveAsync` / `RetrieveMultipleAsync` throw on a fault from a cloned ServiceClient, and record it in the note.</step>
    <step order="1">Consumer inventory: grep `src/**` (ts, tsx, js, cs, yaml, json, ps1; excluding node_modules, dist, bin, obj, out) for `api/dataverse/fetch`, `api/dataverse/record` and `new BffDataverseClient(`. Also check the Copilot agent OpenAPI (`src/solutions/CopilotAgent/spaarke-bff-openapi.yaml`), `scripts/**`, and `docs/**`. Record every hit, and which route each one reaches, in the task note.</step>
    <step order="2">Add the SDK entry point to `DataverseImpersonation.cs`, and switch `UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:147-148` to it.</step>
    <step order="3">Add the fault classifier (reusing `RecordContainerResolver.IsRecordNotFound`), then `ExecuteAsCallerAsync` / `GetRecordAsCallerAsync`. Leave the app-only methods byte-identical.</step>
    <step order="4">Re-wire both handlers: resolve the caller with the probe, refuse on null, call the as-caller method, and map outcomes per the closed set and the ordering rule. Correct the false doc comments and `.WithDescription` strings in FetchEndpoints.cs, RecordEndpoints.cs, FetchService.cs, RecordService.cs and DataverseAuthorizationFilter.cs:29-32. Add one paragraph on the BFF adapter's security model to `docs/architecture/SPAARKE-DATAGRID-FRAMEWORK-ARCHITECTURE.md` (near `:97` / `:316`).</step>
    <step order="5">Tests (the closed set in the acceptance criteria), the new arch guard, and the seeded bite proofs.</step>
    <step order="6">Route guard reconciliation, per the 167 state.</step>
    <step order="7">Build; affected tests, then the full unit, arch and both integration suites, and the BffDataverseClient test; publish size, CVE and conflict checks.</step>
    <step order="8">Write `projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-160-dataverse-proxy-as-caller.md`: inventory, design, the exception type and fault codes (offline evidence, then live), the bite proofs, the guard entries, and the live-gate checklist. Update TASK-INDEX.md: this task's status cell to `✅ [completed]` once the code criteria are met, with the live gate named as pending in the row.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>A consumer, in or out of the repo (a Power Automate flow, the Copilot agent, an Office add-in, a script, a report), depends on these routes returning rows or columns the caller cannot read through Dataverse, for example an admin or reporting view. STOP and present options per CLAUDE.md §6. Do not keep an app-only branch for it.</trigger>
    <trigger>The SDK impersonation path cannot preserve the response shape, or the live gate measures POST /api/dataverse/fetch p50 above the 500 ms target documented at `FetchEndpoints.cs:38` because of the per-request clone and/or the OBO WhoAmI. STOP and report the measurements and the options (for example the cached oid lookup the probe's remarks reject). Do not silently switch to the Web API or OBO transport (shape change), and do not cache an identity across callers.</trigger>
    <trigger>Task 167 has landed and its guard offers no way to record these two routes that its own rules allow (for example it demands a Pending waiver that `NoWaiverIsStale` would reject, or it would require changing `FilterMarker`, `DecisionServices` or `ExplicitlyCreditedFilterTypeNames`). STOP and report to the main session with the exact lines. Do not redesign the guard here.</trigger>
    <trigger>Live, the impersonated read returns the SAME row count as the app-only read for a low-privilege user (NFR-04: equality means impersonation is inert), or the BFF application user lacks `prvActOnBehalfOfAnotherUser`. STOP: the fix is not in effect. Report; do not "fix" it by changing roles.</trigger>
    <trigger>Live, an unknown id and an unreadable id on GET /api/dataverse/record do NOT produce byte-identical bodies (for example one surfaces as a 500 or 400 because its fault arrives as an exception type or code the classifier does not know). Extend the classifier by CODE from the live evidence; if the fault carries no stable code, STOP and report rather than matching message text.</trigger>
    <trigger>The fix appears to need a change to `ExternalModuleDataEndpoints.cs`, to `RecordContainerResolver.cs`, or to the behaviour of the app-only `FetchService.ExecuteAsync` / `RecordService.GetRecordAsync`. STOP: the external seam is owned by tasks 134/157 (contacts cannot be impersonated), and RecordContainerResolver by tasks 155/156.</trigger>
    <trigger>Live, a non-admin internal user loses something they legitimately need through these routes, such as a field-level-secured column a grid shows or the `sprk_gridconfiguration` record their grid loads. STOP and report. Do not widen with an app-only read.</trigger>
    <trigger>adr-check or code-review flags the SDK CallerId impersonation as conflicting with ADR-028 Amendment A5 (whose MUSTs name `RetrieveMultipleImpersonatedAsync` for workforce ROOT SETS), or the probe's OBO WhoAmI as conflicting with A1/A2/A3, or another ADR. Raise it with the CLAUDE.md §6.5 format (path A, B or C) rather than retrying until the check is clean.</trigger>
    <trigger>Step 1 finds no consumer of the internal routes anywhere. Implement the fix anyway, and record the finding in the note for the owner: deleting the routes (the task 073/083 precedent) is an owner decision, not this task's.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseImpersonation.cs:64-124` (the fail-closed impersonation helper, Web API only, no SDK entry point); `UserPrivilegeChecker.cs:143-148` (the only SDK CallerId site, private to the privilege lookup); `DataverseWebApiService.RetrieveMultipleImpersonatedAsync` (`:1030-1067`, Web API, OData row shape); `Infrastructure/Dataverse/IDataverseUserClient.cs` (OBO Web API, OData shape); `CallerRecordAccessProbe.GetCallerSystemUserIdAsync` (`:342-366`, caller identity by OBO WhoAmI); `ISystemUserIdentityResolver` (`Services/Identity/SystemUserIdentityResolver.cs`) and `ICallerSystemUserResolver` (`Services/Ai/Context/CallerSystemUserResolver.cs`), both oid lookups; fault classification: `RecordContainerResolver.IsRecordNotFound` (`:1915-1941`, code-based, not-found only) and the message-based `RecordService.IsRecordNotFound` (`:204-210`), `SavedQueryService.cs:257-259`, `GridConfigurationService.cs:213`, `DataverseServiceClientImpl.cs:367` (all message-based, not-found only). Verified by Grep for `CallerId`, `CallerAADObjectId`, `Impersonated`, `ApplyAsSystemUser`, `0x80040217`, `0x80048306`, `0x80040220`, `Detail.ErrorCode` across `src/server` on 2026-10-02.</existing>
    <extension>Yes, except for one small type. There is no new filter, service, entity-set map or OperationAccessPolicy key. The helper gains ONE SDK entry point next to its two Web API ones. FetchService and RecordService each gain ONE caller-scoped method beside the existing app-only method. Identity reuses the probe. The not-found half of classification reuses `RecordContainerResolver.IsRecordNotFound`. The one new type is the internal static `DataverseCallerFaultClassifier`: no existing predicate recognises the access-denied (0x80048306 / 0x80040220) or impersonation-refused (0x8004A110) codes, the message-based predicates must not be reused (localized messages; they would also have to change the app-only external path, which must stay byte-identical), and adding the codes to `RecordContainerResolver` would edit a file tasks 155/156 own and put access-denied semantics into a container resolver. The only test seams are the two new methods being virtual and the probe's existing virtual member.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any signed-in workforce user with Read at any depth on an entity reads every row of it in the org through POST /api/dataverse/fetch, including secure, other-business-unit and No-Access-walled rows, with all columns, bypassing field-level security. Through GET /api/dataverse/record/{entityLogicalName}/{id} they read any record by id. Both route doc comments claim row-level security that does not exist. Without the code-based classifier, an unreadable record would surface differently from an absent one (a 500 or an echoed 400 versus a 404), which is an existence oracle.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

