  <notes>Sweep findings #9 and #10 (notes/route-authorization-sweep-2026-10-02.md, rows 9 and 10, plus the workspace-misc noteworthy line on the false CallerId doc comments). Owner round 9 (2026-10-03) is the mandate and the fix pattern: reads are decided by the caller's own Dataverse rights through the existing impersonation helper; failures fail closed; unknown and denied ids answer the same. Extending `DataverseImpersonation` with an SDK entry point is how this task "uses the existing helper": the helper had no SDK entry point, and moving these routes to its Web API path would change their response shape. Identity comes from the probe's OBO WhoAmI rather than an oid lookup, per that probe's own reasoning. The sibling `Api/Dataverse` routes (savedquery, metadata, gridconfigurations) were not in the sweep's confirmed class and are not changed here; task 167 classifies them. Field-level security starts applying on these routes as a side effect of impersonation. That is intended, and it is the reason live-gate item (f) exists. Reviewer amendments 2026-10-02 (adversarial review): corrected the fixture anchors; added the record-level access-denied code 0x80048306 (the code a Retrieve of an unreadable row normally returns; the draft had only the table-privilege code, which would have turned denied records into 500s, an existence oracle); made the classifier reuse `RecordContainerResolver.IsRecordNotFound` and owned up to it as the one new type; closed the record handler's ArgumentException / invalid-$select echo paths; named `ICallerSystemUserResolver` as a rejected identity source; rewrote the task-167 branch and trigger so it does not fire on the guard's current, known filter credit; made "app-only never invoked" checkable; added the classifier test file, two bite proofs and live-gate item (j).</notes>

